1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:02,738
2027 starts on a Friday.

2
00:00:03,158 --> 00:00:06,700
And if you're paid every other Friday, that could mean an extra paycheck.

3
00:00:07,120 --> 00:00:16,175
A year that starts on a Friday has 53 Fridays. So one every-other-Friday schedule gets 27 paychecks instead of 26.

4
00:00:16,945 --> 00:00:23,881
And everyone paid every two weeks gets at least two months with three paychecks. Here's exactly which months, and what to do with them.

5
00:00:24,651 --> 00:00:26,288
Step one. Find your months.

6
00:00:26,708 --> 00:00:31,675
If your paydays fall on January 1, 15 and 29, you're on schedule A.

7
00:00:32,095 --> 00:00:37,322
Your 3-paycheck months: January, July and December. 27 paychecks in all.

8
00:00:37,742 --> 00:00:49,606
One catch: January 1 is a holiday, so many employers pay on December 31 instead. Then that check counts toward 2026, and your extra months are July and December.

9
00:00:50,026 --> 00:00:54,291
If your first paydays are January 8 and 22, you're on schedule B.

10
00:00:54,711 --> 00:00:58,743
Your 3-paycheck months: April and October. 26 paychecks.

11
00:00:59,163 --> 00:01:03,921
Paid on another weekday? Your months shift a little. The planner at the end finds yours.

12
00:01:04,341 --> 00:01:14,217
Paid twice a month, like the 15th and the last day? You get exactly 24 paychecks, never a third in one month. This one's for every-two-weeks people.

13
00:01:14,987 --> 00:01:17,865
Step two. Understand why the third check is different.

14
00:01:18,285 --> 00:01:24,900
If you budget by paycheck like we did in episode one, your bills are already covered by the first two checks of the month.

15
00:01:25,320 --> 00:01:32,337
So the third check, $1,850 in our example, has nothing attached to it. Which is exactly why it tends to disappear.

16
00:01:33,107 --> 00:01:35,489
Step three. Give it a job before it lands.

17
00:01:35,909 --> 00:01:37,300
Here's one way to split it.

18
00:01:37,460 --> 00:01:39,086
$1,000 to your debt target.

19
00:01:39,246 --> 00:01:41,322
$500 to your emergency fund.

20
00:01:41,482 --> 00:01:43,608
$165 to sinking funds.

21
00:01:43,768 --> 00:01:47,536
And about 10%, $185, for something fun. You earned it.

22
00:01:47,956 --> 00:01:54,467
Two extra checks is $3,700 this year. On schedule A, with three, it's $5,550.

23
00:01:54,887 --> 00:02:01,109
That's a whole credit card paid off, or a real emergency fund, from money you were never budgeting for anyway.

24
00:02:01,529 --> 00:02:05,769
So, put the dates in your calendar today, and decide the split now, while it's easy.

25
00:02:06,189 --> 00:02:11,429
Then set up the transfers ahead of time, so the money moves on payday, before it can disappear.

26
00:02:12,199 --> 00:02:19,257
The Paycheck Planner in the description now has a paydays tab. Enter your first payday, and it finds your three-paycheck months.

27
00:02:19,677 --> 00:02:22,433
Subscribe for more money math, with real numbers.
